## What

`core`'s `SpaceMapper` now backs shared memory with guest RAM. It no longer uses the kernel's refusing defaults.

- `AddressSpace::allocate_shared` hands out one zeroed run of guest RAM per shared memory. The run comes from the same place as touched pages. RAM is never handed out twice, so the pages read as zero. The run is recorded as a shared allocation. Eden also takes one run (`AllocateAndOpenContinuous` in `KSharedMemory::Initialize`).
- `AddressSpace::map_shared` maps that run at the kernel's address with the kernel's permission. Every page gets its table entry, and its backing in the shadow, at once. The checks run in this order: the range, then whether the pages are exactly one run the space handed out (`SpaceError::NotShared`, new), then what is already mapped, then the tables. A refusal changes nothing.
- `SpaceMapper` implements `Mapper::allocate_shared` and `Mapper::map_shared`. The service's `SharedPages` reach the run through a guest-physical view of the address space.
- Unmapping uses the existing `unmap`: the table entries go and a flush is owed. The pages keep their bytes and are never handed out again. So no page of a shared memory is reused while a mapping of it exists or a flush is pending. The kernel has no call that says when the last reference goes (`memory::shared`, "Not built").
- Running out is `SpaceError::OutOfRam`, which the kernel turns into its documented failure (`SvcError::Internal`, then `InvalidState` from `create_shared_memory`). It never panics. This differs from Eden's `ResultOutOfMemory`; the divergence is listed in the module docs and pinned by a test.
- The startup fixture now gets past `hid`'s `CreateAppletResource` (line 93) and `GetSharedMemoryHandle` (line 95). `STARTUP_MATCHED` goes from 89 to 91. The run now stops at line 97: `GetInfo` info type `0xb` (random entropy), which answers `0x4201` NotImplemented. That belongs to the sibling GetInfo issue. The README's account of where the run stops is updated. The gap list is unchanged: its test regenerates it from the trace and still matches.
- Stale docs: the `docs/architecture.md` provisional row for shared memory is removed, and the PROVISIONAL note in `crates/kernel/src/memory/mapper.rs` is updated (doc comment only).
- `crate::system::host::ONE_VM` is now `pub(crate)`, so the new hypervisor unit test holds the same one-VM lock a run holds.

## Tests

There are 24 new tests: 17 in `crates/core/src/space/tests/shared.rs`, 6 in `crates/core/src/space/tests/kernel.rs` and 1 in `crates/core/src/space/tests/hvf.rs`.

- `shared.rs` covers the address space over flat memory, checked with the software walker:
  - an allocation that exhausts RAM by exactly one page, also with one page already touched, and an overflowing size;
  - a size of one page;
  - a shared mapping that ends exactly where a neighbour starts: the neighbour is unchanged after the shared mapping is unmapped, and one page higher is refused at the neighbour's first page;
  - a shared mapping whose last page is an untouched neighbour (no table entry there), refused by the shadow's check alone;
  - the shadow records the permission asked for, not only the tables;
  - a touch and a host write that the tables refuse for want of table pages take no RAM;
  - a mapping that ends exactly at the end of the address space, and one page past it;
  - pages that are not one shared memory: part of a run, a run plus a page, a touched page, RAM never handed out, and the table pool;
  - the order of checks, with two faults present at once;
  - running out of table pages, which changes nothing;
  - two mappings of one shared memory;
  - `protect`, and `unmap` that keeps the bytes and never reuses the pages;
  - exact error strings.
- `kernel.rs` mirrors 5 of the 15 tests in `crates/kernel/src/svc/shared_memory/tests.rs`, the ones that reach the mapper, through `svc::shared_memory::{map,unmap}_shared_memory` over the real mapper. Its module docs name each one, and give the reason for the other 10: refused before the mapper is called, checking only blocks or reference counts, or checking a different mapper. It covers each permission and a one-page size, host writes and guest writes, refusals that change neither the blocks nor the translation, a mapping ending at the alias-code region's end with a neighbour starting where the other ends, two places, and running out of RAM by exactly one page.
- `hvf.rs` runs a real vCPU (Hypervisor.framework). Guest code at EL0 loads what the service wrote and stores what the service then reads. A store to a read-only mapping is a permission fault and writes nothing. After the unmap and its flush, a load is a translation fault and the pages keep their bytes. Mapped again, the same bytes are seen.

I ran these on this Mac for real (`CI` unset): the hypervisor test and the homebrew startup test. All new tests failed against stub implementations before the real ones. The 6 kernel-level tests also fail when the mapper refuses shared backing. With `STARTUP_MATCHED = 91`, the unchanged code matched 89 lines, so it fails the ratchet.

Mutation checks on `space.rs`: each of these mutations made at least one test fail:

- ignoring the permission;
- dropping the not-shared check, the already-mapped check, the allocation record, the `next` advance or the shadow backing;
- checking room before size;
- `<` changed to `<=` in the room check;
- a wrong physical address in the view;
- (review follow-up) the already-mapped check stopping a page short, the `next` advance in `back` moved before the table entry, and the shadow recording read-write whatever was asked.

An explicit zero-size check survived mutation because it was redundant: `BackingRange::new` already refuses an empty range. I removed it and pinned "zero is Empty even when RAM is used up" with a test.

## Gate

- `cargo xtask ci`: passed. nextest ran 3265 tests: 3265 passed, 1 skipped (after merging current `main`). Doctests passed.
- `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu --locked -- -D warnings`: clean.
- Plain `cargo test -p starquake-core` three times, and nextest over the space and startup tests three times: all passed.

There are no new `#[expect]` attributes and no new dependencies.

## Reachability

In a run, `system::host::run` builds `SpaceMapper(Arc<AddressSpace<PhysicalMemory>>)` as the process's kernel `Mapper`. A server's `IpcContext::create_shared_memory` (hid's `CreateAppletResource`, time:u's `GetSharedMemoryNativeHandle`) calls `Mapper::allocate_shared`, and the guest's `MapSharedMemory` (svc 0x13) calls `Mapper::map_shared`. The homebrew startup fixture exercises this path end to end on the hypervisor.

